   <h3>How the NEPSE Index Is Constructed</h3>
   <p>The NEPSE Index is a market capitalisation-weighted index, meaning that companies with larger market caps contribute more to the index's movement than companies with smaller caps. It began with a base value of 100, established on February 12, 1994, when formal trading commenced. The index is calculated by dividing the aggregate market cap of all listed companies by the base period market cap and multiplying by the base index value of 100.</p>
   <p>Unlike the S\&amp;P 500, which selects 500 companies based on a defined eligibility criteria, or the NIFTY 50, which selects fifty companies representing multiple sectors, the NEPSE Index includes virtually all listed companies. This inclusive approach means that when a new company is listed — whether through an IPO or a merger — it is automatically incorporated into the index. Companies that are suspended from trading are excluded during the suspension period.</p>
