   </div>
   <p>The three shifts covered so far — dematerialization, electronic trading, and ASBA-driven participation — share a common shape. Each removed a specific, identifiable friction or risk that investors of an earlier era had built into their working assumptions about the market. Each took years to fully unfold rather than arriving as a single announcement. And each was, in hindsight, a fairly clear improvement in the mechanics of the market without changing what a share of a company actually represents or what makes one investment sound and another unsound. That last point is the bridge to the rest of this chapter: structural evolution so far has mostly upgraded the pipes, not rewritten the logic of what flows through them. The honest question for a forward-looking investor is whether future evolution will stay in that category, or eventually introduce something that genuinely does require new logic.</p>
