   <p>Where does the NPR 100 face value fit in this triangle? It sits beneath both book value and market price as the floor of the equity accounting structure. A company cannot have a book value per share below zero indefinitely — once accumulated losses exceed total paid-up capital and reserves, the company is technically insolvent. And in that unhappy scenario, the face value of NPR 100 turns into a reminder of what shareholders originally subscribed, now largely or entirely gone.</p>
