   <p>In Nepal's thin secondary debenture market, published prices may not reflect genuine arm's-length transactions. An investor seeking to estimate fair value should discount the bond's cash flows at a yield reflecting: the current T-Bill or Development Bond yield for the relevant maturity (the risk-free rate), plus a credit spread appropriate to the issuer's rating and sector. For an A-rated Nepali commercial bank debenture maturing in five years, a reasonable analytical approach is to take the five-year government bond yield and add 150 to 250 basis points of credit spread. For BBB-rated instruments, 300 to 400 basis points is more appropriate. These are rough guides; in a liquid market, spreads are discovered through active trading, but in Nepal's illiquid environment, they must be estimated through judgment.</p>
