   <h3>The Role of Commodity Exchanges</h3>
   <p>Commodity exchanges serve as organised marketplaces for the buying and selling of standardised physical commodities — agricultural products like rice, wheat, sugar, and spices; metals like gold, silver, and copper; and energy products like crude oil and natural gas. The price discovery and hedging functions of commodity exchanges are economically vital: farmers, processors, exporters, and manufacturers use futures contracts on commodity exchanges to lock in prices and manage the input cost and revenue uncertainty that is inherent in commodity businesses.</p>
   <p>For investors, commodity exchanges offer access to an asset class that behaves differently from equities, providing diversification benefits and, in inflationary environments, a natural store of value. Gold, in particular, has historically served as both an inflation hedge and a crisis hedge in portfolios globally.</p>
