   <p>Microfinance is the part of NEPSE's listed universe where the balance sheet lies about the business. A commercial bank's numbers are, for all their complexity, a fairly faithful mirror of a diversified, collateralized lending operation. A "D" class microfinance institution's numbers are not. Behind a microfinance company's reported 20 percent return on equity can sit ten thousand overlapping, uncollateralized loans made to the same few hundred rural households — households who took a second loan to service the first. Reading an MFI's financials without understanding group lending, joint liability, wholesale funding, and Nepal's own multiple-borrowing crisis is like reading a hospital's revenue statement without knowing which patients are contagious. This chapter builds that understanding from the regulatory architecture up.</p>
