   <p>Let's slow down on the two sections that most investors get wrong: time horizon and risk tolerance, and how they interact.</p>
   <p>A time horizon is simply how long you can leave money invested before you are likely to need it back in cash. This single number does more to determine your correct strategy than almost anything else in this book. Money with a horizon under two or three years has no business in NEPSE equities at all, no matter how confident you feel, because equities can and do stay down for years at a stretch, and you cannot control when your college fee, wedding expense, or medical bill arrives. Money with a horizon of ten, fifteen, twenty years can absorb far more volatility, because time itself becomes a kind of insurance — a bad five-year stretch has historically been followed, in most markets including NEPSE over its multi-decade history, by a recovery, given enough runway for it to happen.</p>
   <p>The mistake to guard against is treating "long term" as a slogan rather than a date. Write an actual year next to every goal. "Retirement" is not a horizon; "2048" is. Once you have real years attached to real goals, the correct allocation for each pool of money becomes far more obvious, almost mechanical, rather than a matter of feeling brave or cautious on a given day.</p>
